// vectors.emp — engine-owned 64-vector table ($000000-$0000FF).
// Every target is an engine symbol; the game contributes nothing here.
//
// ── UNEXPECTED-INTERRUPT POLICY — OWNER-RULED 2026-08-04, DO NOT SILENTLY
// ── REVERT (review item 27, finding 10)
// Every vector the engine does not model — the spurious-interrupt vector ($60),
// the 12 reserved slots ($30-$5C), and IRQ1/2/3/5/7 ($64/$68/$6C/$74/$7C) —
// points at ErrorExcept and HALTS LOUDLY, in BOTH shapes, release included.
// Ruling, verbatim: "A spurious IRQ means a state the engine does not model;
// surface it during development rather than let it corrupt silently."
// Before this the table was internally inconsistent — $60 halted loudly while
// IRQ1/2/3/5/7 took the tolerant `rte` (NullInterrupt) stance — so an
// unmodelled interrupt level either crashed or vanished depending on which
// line the glitch landed on. It is now one policy.
// The release-shape error-handler/MDDBG strip (docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md, "Release-
// shape error handler / MDDBG strip", BLOCKED) may one day remove the vendored
// debugger from release. If it does, these 60 cells must keep pointing at
// SOMETHING that halts loudly (a `bra.s *` freeze, a coloured-border hang, a
// jump to EntryPoint — the owner picks). Repointing them at NullInterrupt's
// `rte` would revert this ruling, and for the fault classes it is actively
// wrong: an `rte` from a bus or address error re-executes the faulting
// instruction and hard-loops.
//
// NullInterrupt intentionally does NOT live in this file — the org-0 image
// is pure data until $200 (vectors $000-$0FF, header $100-$1FF), so
// NullInterrupt is emitted elsewhere (null_interrupt.emp, near the ROM tail).
// It has NO referencer in this table any more (see the policy note above); it
// is kept as the engine's `rte` handler for a future opt-in tolerant vector,
// and its deletion is the blocked strip parcel's call, not this file's.
//
// The 64 entries are raw dc.l link pointers: SYSTEM_STACK is a constants.emp
// equ (folds to the initial SSP); the other 63 are labels resolved at link —
// EntryPoint (boot.emp), the 12 exception stubs (error_handler.emp) and
// VBlank_Handler (vblank.emp) are .emp-owned; the IRQ4 vector points at the
// HBlank_Vector_Slot RAM trampoline.
// Bytes are shape-invariant in
// SOURCE; the VALUES differ per shape through the link (normal). The region's
// fixed 256-byte size makes the header boundary structurally byte-neutral.
//
// FORM — a proc with raw `dc.l <label>`, NOT a `data [*u8; 64]` array (the
// tile_cache.emp BlockStage_PtrTable shape): raw `dc.l <label>` is the right
// capability for this raw CPU-read vector/jump-table class. The corpus
// `data [*u8;N]` pointer
// arrays spell every cell as a quoted label-name string / `extern(...)` /
// `comptime for` — none take a BAREWORD link symbol — so a `data` recast would
// turn these 64 clean cross-module barewords into 64 quoted/extern cells, and
// element 0 (`SYSTEM_STACK`, an integer equ, not a pointer) is heterogeneous
// with the 63 label cells. `dc.l` handles the bareword symbols and the leading
// int uniformly; that is the right form here. (The typed/label-carrying
// data-table construct that would let a `data` array hold barewords + domain
// types is the offset-table-roadmap language ask — gap-ledgered.)
module engine.system.vectors in vectors

pub proc Vectors () clobbers() {
    dc.l    SYSTEM_STACK                    // $00: Initial SSP
    dc.l    EntryPoint                      // $04: Reset PC
    dc.l    BusError                        // $08: Bus error
    dc.l    AddressError                    // $0C: Address error
    dc.l    IllegalInstr                    // $10: Illegal instruction
    dc.l    ZeroDivide                      // $14: Division by zero
    dc.l    ChkInstr                        // $18: CHK exception
    dc.l    TrapvInstr                      // $1C: TRAPV
    dc.l    PrivilegeViol                   // $20: Privilege violation
    dc.l    Trace                           // $24: Trace
    dc.l    Line1010Emu                     // $28: Line 1010
    dc.l    Line1111Emu                     // $2C: Line 1111
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $30: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $34: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $38: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $3C: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $40: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $44: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $48: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $4C: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $50: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $54: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $58: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $5C: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $60: Spurious interrupt
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $64: IRQ1 (unused on MD)
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $68: IRQ2 (external — controller TH)
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $6C: IRQ3 (unused on MD)
    dc.l    HBlank_Vector_Slot              // $70: IRQ4 (HBlank) — RAM jmp-slot trampoline
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $74: IRQ5 (unused on MD)
    dc.l    VBlank_Handler                  // $78: IRQ6 (VBlank)
    dc.l    ErrorExcept                     // $7C: IRQ7 (NMI)
    dc.l    ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap   // $80-$8C: TRAP 0-3
    dc.l    ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap   // $90-$9C: TRAP 4-7
    dc.l    ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap   // $A0-$AC: TRAP 8-11
    dc.l    ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap   // $B0-$BC: TRAP 12-15
    dc.l    ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap   // $C0-$CC: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap   // $D0-$DC: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap   // $E0-$EC: Reserved
    dc.l    ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap, ErrorTrap   // $F0-$FC: Reserved
}
